meta {
  name: Convert to Opus ahead of time
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/transcodes
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "device": "{{deviceId}}",
    "tracks": {{opusTrackIds}}
  }
}

tests {
  test("is accepted at once, with where each FLAC track stands", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(202);
    expect(res.body.queued).to.be.an("array");
    expect(res.body.ready).to.be.an("array");
    expect(res.body.converting).to.be.an("array");
  });
}

docs {
  Has the server convert to Opus, in the background, the tracks a device is about to play: here the FLAC tracks (up to 20) found by **List tracks** (`opusTrackIds`), in their order, next one first. `device` (`deviceId` in the environment) is a UUID that a client generates once and keeps. Answers 202 at once: `queued` will be converted one after the other, `ready` already were, `converting` are being converted right now; MP3 tracks and unknown ids are in none of the lists.

  Each device of an account has its own list, and a request replaces the previous one of the same device only: tracks of the older list that aren't converted yet are dropped. Send an empty `tracks` list to cancel. An account keeps the lists of its 5 most recent devices. Send it again after a few seconds and the queued tracks show up in `ready`. At most 20 tracks (422 beyond), as ids or IRIs (`/api/tracks/{id}`); without a `device`, or with one that isn't a UUID, 422.
}
